# Shared C++ core

## Placement

- Behaviour that both platforms need goes in `libs/`, not in `android/`. Android
  V1 is the launch target, but iOS must be able to adopt the same semantics
  later without reimplementing them. See `DECISIONS.md` SPD-002.
- Session state, GPS sample acceptance, interpolation, pixel storage, area
  assignment, progress calculation, and scoring are shared concerns.
- Platform code drives shared state and reflects it. It does not keep its own
  copy.

## Street Pixels specifics

- Exploration constants are compile-time and named. The 25-metre collection
  radius is not user-configurable (product spec §15.1, §30).
- The collection gate belongs inside the collection routine, not only at the
  caller, so no future caller can bypass it.
- The GPX and track-replay path is separate from live collection. Do not gate it
  on a recording session, and do not let it write live-only state such as
  recency.
- `.pix` and `.pixa` files are mmapped and shared with the renderer. Changing
  their layout requires a format version and a migration.
- Explored state is permanent. Nothing may delete a user's explored pixels
  except an explicit user action.

## Style and build

- Follow `docs/CPP_STYLE.md`. Do not reformat code you are not changing;
  `./tools/unix/clang-format.sh` is the check.
- Register new sources in the owning `CMakeLists.txt`.
- New test targets use `omim_add_test` and are registered with
  `omim_add_test_subdirectory`. Prefer the lightest option set; avoid
  `REQUIRE_QT` and `REQUIRE_SERVER` unless linking genuinely needs them.
- Be careful with concurrency: `StreetStatsDB` uses a recursive mutex, the
  `.pix` mapping uses a shared mutex plus `msync`, and the renderer reads spans
  concurrently.

## Divergence from upstream

This is a CoMaps fork. Every change to `libs/` widens the divergence and raises
future merge cost. Prefer additive modules beside existing code over
restructuring shared engines. If a change to a shared engine seems necessary,
say so and explain why an additive approach will not work.
